\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{frap}
\title{Scrambling, spectra and search coordinates}

\p{Random moves raise a different question from solving: when, and how
quickly, does a scramble lose information about its starting state? A
\newvocab{random walk} depends on its allowed moves and their probabilities.
We use the fixed-frame three-layer move group from [the complete cube
account](frap-0016), and write #{G} for its reachable state group. A walk
with step law #{\mu} has distribution}
##{\Pr[X_k=g]=\mu^{*k}(g)}
\p{from the solved state, where convolution records the successive moves.}

\subtree{
  \title{A parity calculation before mixing claims}

  \p{Let #{\varepsilon:G\to\{+1,-1\}} be the sign of the corner
  permutation. Each face quarter-turn or inverse is a four-cycle on corners,
  so #{\varepsilon=-1}; each face half-turn is a product of two transpositions,
  so #{\varepsilon=+1}. If the walk chooses uniformly from the twelve
  quarter-turns and inverse quarter-turns, then}
  ##{\varepsilon(X_k)=(-1)^k}
  \p{with probability one. Its support is therefore contained in one parity
  coset at every fixed time, while the uniform distribution on #{G} gives equal
  mass to the two cosets. The total-variation distance from uniform is at
  least #{1/2} for every #{k}. This walk cannot converge to uniform: the
  obstruction is periodicity, not a slow estimate of a mixing time.}

  \p{The usual eighteen-move HTM law gives a different exact observable. Six
  half-turns have sign #{+1} and twelve quarter-turns or inverses have sign
  #{-1}, hence one step has}
  ##{\mathbb E[\varepsilon(X_1)]=\frac6{18}-\frac{12}{18}=-\frac13.}
  \p{Independence gives #{\mathbb E[\varepsilon(X_k)]=(-1/3)^k}. Since uniform
  parity has expectation zero, the parity marginal alone gives the lower bound}
  ##{\lVert\mathcal L(X_k)-\mathcal U_G\rVert_{\mathrm{TV}}\geq
      \frac12\left(\frac13\right)^k.}
  \p{This bound decays and does not prove convergence by itself. A lazy walk,
  which includes the identity with positive probability, removes this specific
  periodicity; a convergence statement still needs a generated reachable
  component and an aperiodicity argument.}
}

\subtree{
  \title{Averaging and finite-group spectra}

  \p{For a function #{f:G\to\mathbb C}, the one-step averaging operator is}
  ##{(T_\mu f)(g)=\sum_{s\in G}\mu(s)f(gs).}
  \p{Constants are fixed by #{T_\mu}. For a finite-dimensional unitary
  representation #{\rho}, its matrix coefficients}
  ##{f_{u,v}(g)=\langle u,\rho(g)v\rangle}
  \p{form invariant blocks for this operator: averaging a coefficient replaces
  #{\rho(g)} by multiplication by the Fourier block}
  ##{\widehat\mu(\rho)=\sum_{s\in G}\mu(s)\rho(s).}
  \p{Thus the nonconstant spectrum describes which observables retain memory.
  If the step support generates the reachable group and every nontrivial block
  contracts on the relevant quotient, then these blocks decay and the walk
  converges to uniform on that component. The hypotheses matter: the
  quarter-turn walk above fails the aperiodicity test, so no spectral slogan
  can erase its parity obstruction.}

  \p{The same calculation explains why a prescribed scramble length is not a
  universal guarantee of randomness. A distance bound answers how far a state
  can be from solved; a distributional claim asks how the whole law projects
  onto every persistent observable. These are different certificates.}
}

\subtree{
  \title{Fourier coordinates for learned search}

  \p{Pan and Kondor use irreducible-representation matrix coefficients as a
  Fourier basis for learned value functions on permutation puzzles
  \citet{Sections 4--4.1}{pan2021fourier}. Their experiments compare learned
  scores that choose moves with conventional heuristic and value-based search;
  the paper explicitly discusses the distinction from an admissible lower
  bound \citet{Section 6}{pan2021fourier}. A learned score can rank promising
  moves while overestimating or underestimating the remaining distance. It is
  therefore evidence about search guidance, not a shortest-path certificate.}

  \p{For the cube, a safe lower bound can still be obtained from the exact
  projected distances in a pattern database as in [computer solving and
  efficiency](frap-0018). A Fourier score may be combined with that bound as a
  tie-breaker or move-ordering device, but replacing the bound with the score
  changes the guarantee. The representation coordinates explain how a search
  function is organized; they do not, without an error theorem, make it
  admissible.}
}

\p{This chapter proves only the displayed parity bounds and states the finite
operator framework. It does not claim a full-cube mixing time, a universal
scramble length, or a reproduced machine-learning benchmark. Those would
require a declared move law, reachable component, statistical or spectral
analysis, and independently checked computational evidence.}
